    "prompt": "Below is a description of the Python function `GenerateSchema._model_schema`.\n\nDescription:\n\"\"\"\nThe `_model_schema` function generates a schema for a Pydantic model class. It takes a single parameter, `cls`, which is the model class for which the schema is being generated. The function first checks if a schema for the class already exists and returns it if so. If not, it retrieves the fields and decorators from the class, checks for the existence of certain fields, and wraps the class's configuration. It then generates a schema for the model's fields, including handling any extra fields if allowed by the configuration. If the model is a root model, it processes the root field schema differently. The function applies various validators and serializers to the schema, handles generic models, and finally stores the generated schema in a definitions dictionary. It returns a reference to the generated schema. The function relies on several helper functions and methods, such as `check_decorator_fields_exist`, `generate_schema`, and `apply_model_validators`, to perform its tasks. It raises a `PydanticSchemaGenerationError` if the type annotation for `__pydantic_extra__` is not a `Dict[str, ...]`. The function has side effects by modifying the definitions dictionary and potentially raising exceptions.\n\"\"\"\n\nSplit the description into atomic claims. Each claim must be a single, self-contained factual statement about the function that could be checked against the function's source code (name the function explicitly; no pronouns referring to other claims). Do not add claims that are not in the description; do not merge two facts into one claim. Keep the original wording where possible. Assign each claim exactly one type:\n- \"structural\": the function's signature, parameter names/defaults/types, return type, or which functions, methods, classes or attributes it uses.\n- \"dataflow\": how a value is computed, transformed, passed on, or what is returned for which input.\n- \"behavioural\": exceptions raised or caught, conditions/early returns, side effects, edge-case handling, ordering/iteration behaviour.\n- \"intent\": purpose, rationale, or the role of the function in the larger system.\n\nReturn a JSON array of at most 12 objects: [{\"text\": \"...\", \"type\": \"structural|dataflow|behavioural|intent\"}, ...]",
